==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|soundbite}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsaʊndbaɪt/|nocount=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|journalism|television}} An [[extract]] from a [[speech]] or [[interview]] used as edited into a [[news]] or other broadcast; an interview [[clip]], especially seen as particularly [[expressive]] or [[pithy]].
#: {{synonyms|en|sound cut}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|last=Sagan|first=Carl|authorlink=Carl Sagan|chapter=Science and Hope|title={{w|The Demon-Haunted World|The Demon-Haunted World: Science as a Candle in the Dark}}|url=https://archive.org/details/demonhauntedworl0000unse/|edition=1st|location=New York|publisher={{w|Random House}}|ISBN=0-394-53512-X|pages=25-26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/demonhauntedworl0000unse/page/25|text=The dumbing down of America is most evident in the slow decay of substantive content in the enormously influential media, the 30-second '''sound bites''' (now down to 10 seconds or less), lowest common denominator programming, credulous presentations on pseudo-science and superstition, but especially a kind of celebration of ignorance.}}
# {{lb|en|often|_|pejorative}} A [[one-liner]] deliberately produced for this purpose; a statement specifically intended to be [[punchy]] and [[memorable]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Neil MacFarquhar|title=Saddam Hussein, Defiant Dictator Who Ruled Iraq With Violence and Fear, Dies|work=The New York Times|date=2006-12-30|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/12/30/world/middleeast/saddam-hussein-defiant-dictator-who-ruled-iraq-with.html|issn=0362-4331|passage= The ground offensive against Iraq ended after 100 hours, partly out of concern that American troops not occupy an Arab capital, partly because Arab allies feared the disintegration of Iraq and partly because a “100-hour war” made a good '''sound bite'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Maureen Dowd|authorlink=Maureen Dowd|title=Dark Dark Dark|work=The New York Times|date=2009-02-22|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/02/22/opinion/22dowd.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=President Obama disdains '''sound bites''', and he does not have Bill Clinton’s talent for reducing the abstruse to aperçus.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rowena Mason|title=Lord Steel criticises culture of spin and tweeting in modern politics|work=The Guardian|date=2013-10-31|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2013/oct/31/lord-steel-criticises-culture-spin-doctors-twitter-politics|issn=0261-3077|passage=However, he bemoaned the era of rehearsed '''soundbites''' and "Prime Minister's Insult Time", which he said have diminished parliament.}}
# {{synonym of|en|sound clip}}

